---
/**
 * /roadmap — ported from the legacy `roadmap.html`.
 *
 * ── OA-13, AND WHY THE FIX IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN A REWORDING ────────────
 *
 * The defect was Phase 3 carrying the date pill "Q4 2026" directly above its
 * own sentence "Early research, not yet committed engineering". A reader takes
 * the date and ignores the caveat, and the date is what gets quoted back.
 *
 * Rewording that one pill would fix that one pill. What is done here instead is
 * that `date` is optional on the Phase type in src/data/roadmap.ts and `status`
 * is not, so a band gets a quarter only when somebody is committing to it and
 * everything else has to say what it is in words. The defect cannot be typed
 * back in without noticing.
 *
 * TWO SIGNALS, BOTH IN TEXT. Every phase and every "where the AI is heading"
 * row prints its status as a sentence fragment a reader can read out loud:
 * "Live now", "In progress", "Researching, not committed". The colour is a
 * second signal and never the only one, the same rule the timeline in
 * about.astro follows. WCAG 1.4.1.
 *
 * ── WHAT CHANGED FROM THE LEGACY PAGE, AND WHY ──────────────────────────────
 *
 *   Phase 3's date      "Q4 2026" -> no date at all, and the pill says "No
 *                       committed date". Carried from the legacy source, which
 *                       was already corrected on 2026-08-02.
 *   The "Later" pill    "2027" removed. It sat above "on the radar beyond the
 *                       next twelve months", which is a horizon rather than a
 *                       plan; a year printed beside an item reads as a delivery
 *                       year however carefully it is hedged. The band is called
 *                       "Later" and the lead says what later means.
 *   Broader SSO         Narrowed to OIDC. SAML 2.0 with SCIM provisioning is
 *                       SHIPPED (web/app/api/auth/sso/_lib/saml-config.ts,
 *                       web/app/api/scim/v2/**), and /pricing already publishes
 *                       it, so the roadmap was contradicting the pricing page.
 *                       The false tier claim, "available now on Portfolio", went
 *                       with it: pricing states SSO with no tier attached
 *                       because the repo cannot answer that question.
 *   The trial claim     "Every plan includes a 14-day trial once you're
 *                       approved" removed from the closing band, and BETA-MODE.md
 *                       forbids beta language on this site besides. Two pages
 *                       cannot publish two different offers.
 *
 *                       AND THE SITE HAS NOW CHOSEN WHICH OFFER SURVIVES. When
 *                       this page dropped the claim, /pricing was still
 *                       publishing it, so the inconsistency was resolved on one
 *                       page and left standing on the other. Owner decision
 *                       A-54, 2026-08-04, deleted the trial from every marketing
 *                       surface: it is a commercial commitment nobody intended
 *                       to honour, CrowMark is sold by scoped engagement with
 *                       "Book a demo" as the CTA, and access is offered by
 *                       request with no card taken. Nothing on this page needed
 *                       to change for that; it is recorded here so a future
 *                       reader does not restore the sentence believing /pricing
 *                       still carries it.
 *   Last updated        Restamped, because this page materially changed today.
 *                       A "last updated" that does not move when the document
 *                       does is worse than none.
 *   The arrows          The legacy timeline drew its structure with CSS and
 *                       reveal animations driven by three scripts. It is an
 *                       <ol> here, so the order is in the markup and a screen
 *                       reader gets the sequence rather than four unordered
 *                       boxes.
 *
 * ── WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT CHANGED, AND IS REPORTED INSTEAD ───────────────
 *
 * A read of the platform repo suggests two Phase 2 and Phase 3 items may
 * already ship: passkeys exist as an MFA factor (web/lib/auth/passkey-ceremony.ts,
 * web/app/api/account/passkeys/**) and two regulatory-monitor implementations
 * are scheduled (api/app/services/regulatory_monitor.py,
 * api/app/services/watchtower_service.py). Neither is re-phased here. Whether a
 * feature is "shipped" for the purposes of a public commitment document is a
 * question about production enablement and customer visibility, not about
 * whether a file exists, and moving an item UP the roadmap on that evidence
 * would be an agent promoting work the owner has not announced. Both are raised
 * in OWNER-ACTIONS.md. Understating is the safe direction; overstating is not.
 *
 * ── NO JAVASCRIPT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The legacy page loaded GSAP, ScrollTrigger, a roadmap-reveal module, a motion
 * choreographer and a reveal failsafe: five scripts, whose combined job was to
 * fade in content that is already there. All of it is rendered at first paint.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../components/layout/Section.astro';
import Button from '../components/ui/Button.astro';
import { breadcrumbs } from '../lib/schema';
import {
  PHASES,
  STATUS_LABEL,
  AI_LIVE,
  AI_NEXT,
  METHOD,
  WONT_BUILD,
} from '../data/roadmap';

const title = 'CrowMark roadmap | Shipped, in flight and next | CrowAgent';
const description =
  'See what CrowAgent has shipped, what is in flight, and what is coming next for CrowMark, across public sector tenders and private sector bids.';

const REQUEST_ACCESS = '/contact?enquiry=limited-access#contact-form';

/* The date this document last materially changed. Written once and rendered in
   both the machine-readable attribute and the visible text, so the two cannot
   drift the way a hand-typed pair always eventually does. */
const LAST_UPDATED = '2026-08-03';
const LAST_UPDATED_LABEL = '3 August 2026';

const schema = [
  breadcrumbs([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Roadmap', path: '/roadmap' },
  ]),
];
---

<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/roadmap"
  image="/Assets/og/roadmap.png?v=20260730"
  schema={schema}
>
  <Section
    as="h1"
    eyebrow="Roadmap"
    title="CrowAgent product roadmap"
    standfirst="What is shipped, what is in flight, and what comes next for CrowMark. Research is labelled as research, and dates on unshipped work are indicative."
    id="hero"
  >
    <p class="updated">
      Future-phase dates are indicative and subject to change. Every shipped feature is
      announced on the <a href="/changelog">changelog</a>.
    </p>
    {/*
      `.meta`, NOT `.updated`, FROM 2026-08-04.

      These two paragraphs look alike and are not the same object. The one above
      is a body note — a sentence about how to read the dates on this page — and
      `.updated` is the right class for it. This one is PROVENANCE: when the
      document last changed. /privacy, /cookies and /terms all state exactly that
      fact, and all three render it as `.legal__stamp`, which is the shared bare
      mono micro-label from styles/labels.css. This page rendered the same fact in
      the proportional body face, sentence case, no tracking, in a different
      colour — one fact, two treatments, and the fork was invisible because the
      two pages are never on screen together.

      `.meta` is one of the three label recipes check-treatments.js allows, so
      this adds no fourth; it joins the one that already exists. The class is on
      the <p> rather than restated as CSS, which is the whole point.
    */}
    <p class="meta">
      Last updated: <time datetime={LAST_UPDATED}>{LAST_UPDATED_LABEL}</time>
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    eyebrow="The build, in order"
    title="Shipped, in progress, and next"
    standfirst="A single timeline from what is live in production today to the research shaping the next twelve months. Each phase is anchored to a specific UK or EU regulatory driver, so the plan is deterministic, not speculative."
    id="timeline"
  >
    <ol class="tl" role="list">
      {
        PHASES.map((p) => (
          <li class:list={['ph', 'tl__item']}>
            {/* THE MARKER, and it is the same object /about's history draws.
                Both live in styles/timeline.css: the rail belongs to the <ol>
                and the dot is pulled back onto it by arithmetic rather than by
                a number that has to be retyped when the gutter clamps. The only
                thing decided here is what the stop MEANS, which is the one
                question a shared file cannot answer. */}
            <span class:list={['tl__dot', `tl__dot--${p.status}`]} aria-hidden="true" />
            <div class="ph__head">
              <h3 class="ph__name">{p.name}</h3>
              {/* Status as words. A reader who cannot see the colour, or who is
                  reading this printed, still knows which band is which. */}
              <p class:list={['chip', 'ph__status', `ph__status--${p.status}`]}>{STATUS_LABEL[p.status]}</p>
              {/* A quarter ONLY where it is a commitment. Where there is none,
                  the page says so rather than leaving a gap somebody fills in
                  with a guess. This is the OA-13 fix. */}
              <p class="meta ph__date">{p.date ?? 'No committed date'}</p>
            </div>

            <p class="ph__lead">{p.lead}</p>

            {/* These items carried `surface--read` until 2026-08-03, on the
                argument that a four-line paragraph describing a capability is
                running prose rather than a scanned label. The owner reversed
                that decision; the opt-out is retired sitewide and the body
                centres with the heading. See styles/alignment.css. */}
            {/* `.card-row`, NOT A LOCAL GRID, and it is the fix for the second
                half of the owner's report: cards of identical height whose two
                "View product" links sat 28.9px apart. Stretch equalises the
                BOXES — the grid already did that — and leaves the content inside
                them wherever the copy happened to end. styles/card-row.css says
                so itself and hands the answer over: `card-grow` on the child
                that should absorb the slack, which here is the body paragraph,
                so the call to action is pushed to the card's floor instead of
                floating up behind a short sentence. `--pair` because a band with
                one item must not spread that item across both tracks. */}
            <ul class="ph__items card-row card-row--pair" role="list">
              {p.items.map((it) => (
                <li class="item surface surface--pad">
                  <h4 class="item__h">{it.title}</h4>
                  <p class="item__body card-grow">{it.body}</p>
                  {it.href && (
                    <p class="item__more">
                      <a href={it.href}>View product</a>
                    </p>
                  )}
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </Section>

  <Section
    eyebrow="The intelligence layer"
    title="How the AI actually works"
    standfirst="CrowMark is not a chatbot wrapped around a regulation. It runs a deterministic scoring engine first; large language models do the writing and explaining on top, grounded in the named statute. The model proposes a draft, you review and sign it off."
    id="intelligence"
  >
    <ul class="grid grid--2" role="list">
      {
        AI_LIVE.map((c) => (
          <li class="card surface surface--pad">
            <p class="meta card__flag">Live today</p>
            <h3 class="item__h">{c.heading}</h3>
            <p class="item__body">
              {c.body}
              {c.link && (
                <>
                  {' '}
                  <a href={c.link.href}>{c.link.label}</a>.
                </>
              )}
            </p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <h3 class="sub">Where the AI work is heading</h3>
    <ul class="next" role="list">
      {
        AI_NEXT.map((n) => (
          <li class="next__row">
            <p class:list={['chip', 'ph__status', `ph__status--${n.status}`]}>{STATUS_LABEL[n.status]}</p>
            <div>
              <h4 class="item__h">{n.heading}</h4>
              <p class="item__body">{n.body}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <p class="note">
      We will not ship a model that free-styles the law. Every AI feature keeps a deterministic
      engine underneath, and narrative generation is the last step, never the source. That is a
      fixed design rule, not a phase.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    eyebrow="Methodology"
    title="How we decide what to ship next"
    standfirst="Every CrowAgent product traces back to a UK or EU regulation that already exists or has already passed Royal Assent. No speculative tools, and no pivot to whatever is loudest this quarter."
    id="method"
  >
    <ol class="method" role="list">
      {
        METHOD.map((m) => (
          <li class="method__row">
            <p class="method__n" aria-hidden="true">{m.n}</p>
            <div>
              <h3 class="item__h">{m.heading}</h3>
              <p class="item__body">{m.body}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </Section>

  <Section
    eyebrow="Scope discipline"
    title="What we won't build"
    standfirst="Honesty about scope is part of the deal. We actively decide against patterns that diverge from our regulatory anchor."
    id="wont-build"
  >
    <ul class="grid grid--2" role="list">
      {
        WONT_BUILD.map((w) => (
          <li class="card surface surface--pad">
            <h3 class="item__h">{w.heading}</h3>
            <p class="item__body">{w.body}</p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section
    eyebrow="Regulatory clock"
    title="Deadlines on the radar"
    standfirst="The dates the plan above is anchored to, with the instrument each one comes from."
    id="clock"
  >
    <ul class="clock" role="list">
      <li class="clock__row">
        <p class="meta clock__when">Feb 2025</p>
        <div>
          <h3 class="item__h">PPN 002 Social Value (mandatory from 1 October 2025)</h3>
          <p class="item__body">
            A minimum 10% social value weighting applies to in-scope central government
            contracts. CrowMark maps each bid to the PPN 002 missions, M1 to M5, and the eight
            policy outcomes, using a curated catalogue of TOMs-aligned measures.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="note">
      PPN 002 was published on 13 February 2025 and has been mandatory since 1 October 2025.
      Both dates, and the instruments behind them, are on <a href="/sources">sources</a>.
    </p>
  </Section>

  {/* THE CLOSING CTA IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN A CARD.
      Briefly converted to FinalCta.astro on 2026-08-04 and reverted the same
      day on the owner's correction: "Get started CTA must not be in card."
      Recorded rather than silently undone, because the shared FinalCta panel is
      the default closing block on /, /about, /tools and both tool pages, so a
      bare closing section here reads as an oversight unless the intent is
      written down. It is the intent. /roadmap ends on a plain ask because the
      page it closes is a statement of what is NOT built yet, and a raised,
      lit panel would sell harder than the page has earned. */}
  <Section
    eyebrow="Get started"
    title="Request access to CrowMark"
    standfirst="Access is by request while we onboard teams one at a time. Tell us what you bid for and we will come back to you."
    id="cta"
  >
    <div class="btn-row">
      <Button href={REQUEST_ACCESS}>Request access</Button>
      <Button href="/contact" variant="secondary">Enterprise enquiry</Button>
    </div>
  </Section>
</Base>

<style>

  /* RENAMED FROM `.meta`, 2026-08-04. styles/labels.css now owns `.meta` as the
     one bare mono micro-label, and this is a different object: a centred body
     note on the 62ch measure. Two objects, one name, and the shared rule was
     quietly restyling this one. */
  .updated {
    margin: 0 auto var(--stack-1);
    max-width: 62ch;
    text-align: center;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }
  .meta:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  /*
   * `.updated a` ADDED 2026-08-04, and it is a real defect rather than a tidy-up.
   *
   * The "changelog" link in the body note above this block was in none of these
   * lists, so it took no colour from the site at all and fell back to the USER
   * AGENT's dark-scheme link colour. Measured on the built page at 1440:
   * rgb(158, 158, 255) — Chromium's #9E9EFF — against rgb(255, 255, 255) for every
   * other prose link on the other 42 routes. It was the only link on the site
   * whose colour nobody had chosen, and it read as a stray browser default because
   * that is exactly what it was.
   *
   * `.updated` was split off `.meta` on 2026-08-04 with a written argument (it is a
   * body note, not a micro-label) and the split took the type with it and left the
   * link colour behind. That is the same shape as every other finding tonight, in
   * miniature: a rename that moves one half of a rule.
   */
  .meta a,
  .note a,
  .updated a,
  .item__body a {
    color: var(--c-interactive);
  }
  .meta a:focus-visible,
  .note a:focus-visible,
  .updated a:focus-visible,
  .item__body a:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--c-interactive); outline-offset: 3px; }

  /* Running explanation, on the section body rather than in a card. Cards on
     this site centre their content because they are scanned; a closing
     paragraph is read. */
  .note {
    margin: var(--sec-gap) auto 0;
    max-width: 72ch;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  /* ── The timeline ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     An <ol>, so the order of the phases is a fact in the markup rather than a
     line drawn down the left in CSS. The rule below is decoration on top of a
     structure that already reads correctly with no styling at all.

     THE RAIL AND THE MARKERS ARE styles/timeline.css, FROM 2026-08-04, and the
     reason is A-13. This page drew the rail as `border-left` on `.ph` — on each
     ITEM — inside a grid with `gap: var(--sec-gap)`. Measured on the built page
     at 1440: four rails of 522, 394, 423 and 365px with 60px of nothing between
     them, and no markers anywhere. That is not a spine, it is four brackets, and
     no value would have fixed it: a border on a gapped grid item can only ever
     draw the item, while the thing a timeline draws is the relationship BETWEEN
     items. It belongs to the list, which is exactly where /about had it. Shared
     rather than copied, so there is one of them now instead of a better one and
     a worse one. */
  .tl {
    gap: var(--sec-gap);
    /*
     * THE RAIL FADES, AND THAT IS THE ONE THING ADOPTED FROM :8092.
     *
     * The reference build painted its rail
     * `linear-gradient(#2DD4BF, rgba(12,201,168,.35) 55%, rgba(255,255,255,.08))`
     * — full strength at the live band, nearly gone by the time the plan is
     * research. It is the page's own argument drawn as a line, and it is the
     * kind of finishing the owner pointed at, so it is rebuilt here from role
     * tokens rather than the reference's three literal colours.
     *
     * It is a SECOND signal and never the only one. Every band still prints
     * "Live now", "In progress" or "Researching, not committed" in words, which
     * is the rule this page has followed from the start. WCAG 1.4.1.
     *
     * /about keeps the flat default: a company history is not less certain at
     * the bottom than at the top, so a fade there would say something untrue.
     */
    --tl-rail: linear-gradient(
      var(--c-verified) 0%,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-verified) 35%, transparent) 45%,
      var(--c-border) 100%
    );

    /* A phase leads with a bare heading rather than a padded card, so its marker
       lines up with the cap height of that heading instead of a card's padding.
       `--t-h4` at line-height 1.2 puts the optical centre of the first line a
       little over half a line down; half the dot is then taken back off so the
       dot is centred on it rather than hanging from it. */
    --tl-dot-top: calc(var(--t-h4) * 0.6 - var(--tl-dot) / 2);
  }

  .tl__dot--live {
    background: var(--c-verified);
    box-shadow: 0 0 14px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-verified) 55%, transparent);
  }
  /* The dot takes the colour of the words beside it, so the two signals cannot
     disagree. These mirror `.ph__status--*` below, deliberately. */
  .tl__dot--progress { background: var(--c-text-sub); }
  .tl__dot--research { background: var(--c-text-muted); }

  .ph__head {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 10px 14px;
    margin-bottom: var(--stack-1);
  }

  .ph__name {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h4);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  /*
   * STATUS IS A WORD IN A CAPSULE, AND THE CAPSULE IS NOT THE MESSAGE.
   * Strip every colour off this page and each band still says "Live now",
   * "In progress" or "Researching, not committed" in full. The colour only
   * makes the same fact quicker to find.
   */
  .ph__status {
    margin: 0;
    white-space: nowrap;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }
  .ph__status--live { color: var(--c-verified); border-color: var(--c-border-glow); }
  .ph__status--progress { color: var(--c-text-sub); }

  .ph__date {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  .ph__lead {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-2);
    max-width: 72ch;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  /* `minmax(0, 1fr)`, never a bare `1fr`. A grid item's default min-width is
     min-content, and these cards hold long unbroken product names. */
  .grid {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(18px, 2.2vw, 30px);
  }
  .grid--2 { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }

  /* `.ph__items` OWNS NOTHING BUT ITS LIST RESET NOW. Its two tracks, its gap
     and the floor its cards share all come from `.card-row card-row--pair`, so
     the only thing left to say here is that it is a list. Restating the columns
     would put the recipe back in two places, which is the whole thing the
     shared file exists to stop. */
  .ph__items {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .item,
  .card { min-width: 0; }

  .card__flag {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    color: var(--c-ambient-start);
  }

  .item__h {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h4);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .item__body {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  .item__more { margin: var(--stack-2) 0 0; }
  /* WCAG 2.2 target size. This link sits alone on its own line rather than
     inline in a sentence, so the 2.5.8 inline exception does not apply. */
  .item__more a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: var(--btn-h-sm);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    color: var(--c-interactive);
  }
  .item__more a:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--c-interactive); outline-offset: 3px; }

  /* ── Where the AI work is heading ────────────────────────────────────────*/
  .sub {
    margin: var(--sec-gap) 0 var(--stack-2);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    line-height: var(--lh-tight);
    font-size: var(--t-h4);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .next,
  .method,
  .clock {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--stack-2);
  }

  .next__row,
  .method__row,
  .clock__row {
    display: grid;
    /* THE LABEL COLUMN MUST NOT BE NARROWER THAN ITS OWN LABEL.
       Owner report 2026-08-04: "Researching, not committed" was printing on top
       of the headings beside it, on two rows of the AI section.
       MEASURED at 1440: the track resolved to 192px (12rem) while that chip
       renders 254px, because `.chip` is `white-space: nowrap` — correctly, a
       chip is a single-line label and labels.css owns that. So the chip
       overflowed its own track by 62px, and since the heading column starts at
       408px and the chip ran to 442px, it painted 34px into the heading. The
       shorter "In progress" chip is 122px, fits, and never overlapped, which is
       why only two of the three rows showed it.
       `minmax(max-content, 12rem)` reads oddly and is exactly right: when
       max-content exceeds the 12rem maximum the minimum wins, so the track
       becomes as wide as the widest chip and never less. Short labels still get
       the 12rem column the design intended.

       AND THAT ALONE LEAVES THE COLUMN RAGGED, which is worth writing down
       because the first version of this comment claimed the opposite. Each
       <li> is its OWN grid container, so the tracks are NOT shared down the
       list: measured after the first fix, the 122px "In progress" row kept a
       192px track while the two 254px "Researching" rows grew to 254.219px, so
       one heading started at 408px and the others at 470px. The overlap was
       gone and the column was crooked.
       `subgrid` on the row is what makes the list one grid: the parent owns the
       two tracks and every row borrows them, so the widest chip anywhere sets
       the column for all of them and the headings share an edge. The row stays
       a real element rather than `display: contents`, because it carries the
       border-bottom that separates the entries. Same mechanism styles/card-row.css
       already uses for .card-sync. */
       Fixing the TRACK rather than the chip keeps the fix where the defect is:
       the chip was the right width for its text; the column was the wrong width
       for the chip. */
    grid-template-columns: subgrid;
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    gap: clamp(14px, 2vw, 28px);
    align-items: start;
    padding-bottom: var(--stack-2);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--c-border);
  }

  /* The lists own the tracks; the rows above borrow them. This is the half that
     makes the label column straight rather than merely non-overlapping — see the
     note on .next__row. `max-content` here is computed across EVERY row, so the
     widest status chip in the list sets the column for all of them. */
  .next,
  .method,
  .clock {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(max-content, 12rem) minmax(0, 1fr);
  }
  .next__row:last-child,
  .method__row:last-child,
  .clock__row:last-child { border-bottom: 0; padding-bottom: 0; }

  /* The status capsule inside a row hugs its text rather than filling the
     column, or a two-word label would sit in a 12rem box. */
  .next__row > .ph__status { justify-self: start; }

  /*
   * THE STEP NUMERAL, AND ITS COLOUR IS A FIX RATHER THAN A CHOICE.
   *
   * It was --c-border, which is rgba(160, 180, 228, 0.18) and the same value the
   * legacy page used (`text-ca-bg/10`). axe-core reported it as a SERIOUS
   * color-contrast failure at both 1440 and 390, four nodes, and it was right:
   * against --c-bg that lands near 1.3:1 where large bold text needs 3:1.
   *
   * `aria-hidden` does not excuse it. The attribute is correct here, because the
   * numbers repeat ordering an <ol> already carries, but hiding text from a
   * screen reader does nothing for a sighted reader with low vision, who is
   * exactly the person the rule protects. A border token was being used as a
   * text colour, which is the actual mistake.
   *
   * --c-numeral-far mixed 78% into the page reads about 4.7:1: still a quiet
   * background numeral, and now one that can be read at all. Composed from
   * tokens rather than typed as a hex, so it moves if the palette does.
   */
  .method__n {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-counter);
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1;
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-numeral-far) 78%, var(--c-bg));
  }

  .clock__when {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--c-ambient-start);
  }

  /* `.ph__items` USED TO BE IN THIS LIST and is deliberately not any more. It
     is a `.card-row` now, and card-row.css stands its own columns down at 56rem
     with the argument written out beside the rule. Restating a second, earlier
     breakpoint here would mean the row stacked at one width on this page and
     another everywhere else, which is the fork the shared file exists to stop.
     `.grid--2` is not a card row — it holds four cards, not two — so it keeps
     its own. */
  @media (max-width: 1023px) {
    .grid--2 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  }

  @media (max-width: 719px) {
    .next__row,
    .method__row,
    .clock__row { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--stack-1); }
    .method__n { font-size: var(--t-h3); }
  }
</style>
